              "Trespass to person is classified into three classic forms:<br/><strong>1. Assault (Creating Reasonable Apprehension):</strong><br/>• Assault is an act of the defendant which causes a reasonable apprehension of an imminent battery in the mind of the plaintiff.<br/>• Physical contact is NOT necessary. Words alone are usually not assault unless accompanied by threatening gestures.<br/>• <em>Example:</em> Pointing a loaded gun at someone, shaking a fist in their face within hitting distance, or raising a stick.<br/>• <em>Essential:</em> The defendant must have the apparent immediate capability to carry out the threat.",
              "<strong>2. Battery (Application of Unlawful Force):</strong><br/>• Battery is the intentional, direct application of force to another person without consent or lawful excuse.<br/>• Force does not need to be violent or painful. Even spitting on someone, cutting someone's hair without consent, or splashing water is battery.<br/>• In <em>Cole v. Turner (1704)</em>, Lord Holt famously declared: <em>\"The least touching of another in anger is a battery.\"</em>",
              "<strong>3. False Imprisonment (Total Restraint of Liberty):</strong><br/>• False imprisonment is the total restraint of a person's bodily liberty for any period of time, without legal authority or lawful justification.<br/>• Actual physical prison walls or chains are NOT needed. Locking someone in a room, detaining them in a moving car, or using coercive authority to prevent them from leaving an open street constitutes false imprisonment.<br/>• <em>Crucial Element:</em> The restraint must be <strong>TOTAL</strong>. If a reasonable means of escape is available, it is not false imprisonment."
