              "<strong>Partial Obstruction vs. Total Restraint (Bird v. Jones):</strong><br/>In the historic case of <em>Bird v. Jones (1845)</em>, where part of a public footway on Hammersmith Bridge was partitioned off with seats for watching a boat race, the plaintiff was prevented from passing along that footpath but was free to go in the opposite direction. The court held that partial obstruction of movement does not constitute false imprisonment; imprisonment requires total and complete confinement within defined boundaries."
