              "In <em>Nilabati Behera v. State of Orissa (1993)</em>, where a youth died in police custody, the Supreme Court clarified that the doctrine of sovereign immunity applies only to private law tort claims. In public law, sovereign immunity is completely unavailable against state infringement of Article 21. The payment of monetary compensation by constitutional courts is not exemplary damages, but an acknowledged constitutional remedy for the violation of inalienable human dignity."
